---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DateCalculator from '@/components/tools/datetime/DateCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="date-calculator">
  <DateCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to add or subtract time from a date</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Set the start date. The default is today.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Add to date</strong> or <strong>Subtract from date</strong>.</li>
      <li>
        Fill in any mix of years, months, weeks and days, or tap a preset such as 30 days or 6 months.
      </li>
      <li>
        Turn on <strong>Business days mode</strong> when the offset must skip weekends, for example for
        "10 working days from receipt" deadlines.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The step-by-step panel shows each intermediate date, so you can see where a month-end clamp or a weekend
      skip changed the result.
    </p>

    <h2>The order of operations matters</h2>
    <p>
      Units are applied largest first: years, then months, then weeks, then days. This is the standard convention,
      and it matters at month boundaries. From 31 January:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Plus 1 month, then plus 1 day, gives 1 March (28 February, then one day).</li>
      <li>Plus 1 day, then plus 1 month, also gives 1 March, but only by coincidence.</li>
      <li>
        From 30 January the two orders give different results: plus 1 month gives 28 February, and one more day
        gives 1 March; plus 1 day gives 31 January, and one more month gives 28 February.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Date arithmetic is not associative around month ends, so always state the order. This date calculator applies
      the standard order and shows it.
    </p>

    <h2>Month-end clamping</h2>
    <p>
      When the target month is too short for the day number, the result is clamped to the last day of that month.
      Almost all date libraries, spreadsheets and contract drafting conventions do this.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Start date</th>
            <th>Operation</th>
            <th>Result</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>31 January 2027</td>
            <td>+ 1 month</td>
            <td>28 February 2027</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>31 January 2028</td>
            <td>+ 1 month</td>
            <td>29 February 2028 (leap year)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>31 March 2026</td>
            <td>− 1 month</td>
            <td>28 February 2026</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>29 February 2028</td>
            <td>+ 1 year</td>
            <td>28 February 2029</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>31 May 2026</td>
            <td>+ 3 months</td>
            <td>31 August 2026</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The operation is not symmetric: 31 January plus one month is 28 February, but 28 February minus one month is
      28 January, not 31 January. Clamping cannot be reversed, so calculate long chains of month arithmetic from the
      original start date, not step by step.
    </p>

    <h2>Business days mode</h2>
    <p>
      In business-days mode, only Monday to Friday count. The start day itself never counts, so "3 business days
      from Monday" is Thursday. If the start date is on a weekend, the count starts from the next Monday. Public
      holidays are not applied here; the business days calculator handles them, with a holiday list you can edit.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently needed offsets</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Offset</th>
            <th>Where it shows up</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>14 days</td>
            <td>EU consumer right to cancel an online order</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>30 days</td>
            <td>Standard invoice payment terms, notice periods</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>45 and 180 days</td>
            <td>US 1031 exchange identification and closing windows</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>90 days</td>
            <td>Schengen and many visa-free stay limits, probation periods</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>180 days</td>
            <td>Passport validity commonly required for entry</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>280 days</td>
            <td>Estimated due date from the last menstrual period</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Rules given in days almost always exclude the start date. When a rule says "within X days of", read the
      source carefully: some count clear days, some count business days.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
